/** siman 069 part5 — peleti (22), yad-avraham (15), yad-ephraim (19) */
export const TRANSLATIONS_PART5 = {
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 69 seif 1) If after rinsing he cut each piece into two or three, he must rinse them; and if he did not do so it is as if he did not rinse. The reason in Mordechai: through knife pressure it expels visible blood. According to my reasoning he holds the reason for initial rinsing is visible blood on the surface, that salt becomes full from it and does not expel blood; therefore it seems sufficient with thorough rinsing without soaking, for the meat is already softened, and one need only remove the visible blood from the place he cut. Now it is clear there is no contradiction with what the hagahah wrote at the end of this siman seif 20, that one may cut during the salting period if he wishes to rinse there and return to salt there — see there and examine. And so too in Lechem HaPanim and Bedek HaBayit. And if he cut only from one side, presumably there is sixty as I wrote.`,
    '2#_': `(Seif 2) If he salted without rinsing. Plain unsalted meat touching a salted piece has the law of unsalted meat salted without rinsing, for salt absorbs into it the visible blood on it; but plain unsalted meat that fell into brine — brine does not absorb the visible blood on its surface and it is permitted. One may learn this from what Shach wrote siman 70 s.k. 29 — see there. And it is not comparable to a subsidiary piece that fell among salted pieces in an unperforated vessel, which is permitted, for there the subsidiary does not expel through contact with the salted piece and the blood in it did not spread from place to place. See in our words there siman 70. However, in Mei Yisrael general rule 6 s.k. 4 it implies like Ohr HaChaim that one permits even if it mixed without rinsing among salted pieces; and in any case, when it touches only from one side it is permitted as I wrote. Darkei Moshe wrote: if the meat was soaked in water beforehand, it is like meat cooked without final rinsing and requires sixty against the blood and salt on it as siman 69. And this is obvious. And the law of roasting — see below siman 76, that l'chatchila one needs rinsing and b'dieved it is permitted in every case. And in Mei Yisrael general rule 5 s.k. 5 he explained that it is permitted only where it was not salted much and did not remain the salting period — see there. And goose fat without first rinsing — Bedek HaBayit s.k. 52 permitted it in great loss. And if it remained three days without rinsing, it seems one should permit even without great loss, unlike what seems from Mei Yisrael general rule 4 s.k. 5 — see there. And in siman 75 he permits goose fat without first rinsing for roasting; and if it did not remain the salting period or was salted only a little, even for cooking. And according to what I wrote in this siman, namely that it was not yet cooked and there is no loss in roasting it, if there is great loss even for cooking it is permitted, and all the more if it was already cooked that there is no prohibition. And Pri Megadim is lenient regarding goose fat without first rinsing and stringent if it remained three days. And see Noda BiYehudah siman 24, who forbids goose fat as well if it remained three days without rinsing. However, without final rinsing, Ohr HaChaim in Rokeach general rule 11 forbids even goose fat.`,
    '3#_': `(Seif 6 in the hagahah) Regarding honoring guests, he wrote in Sulam LaMinchah in the name of Torat HaEsh that wherever "for honoring guests" is mentioned, they are those fit for honor, and paupers who pass through for scraps and food are in the category of charity and not in the category of honoring guests. He further wrote that l'chatchila one should not invite his friend when he will need to be lenient on his account; but if he already invited, one may be lenient even for townspeople. And regarding great loss he wrote there in general rule 38 that everything is according to the estimation of the posek's mind according to time and place — the rich according to his wealth and the poor according to his poverty — and also according to the matter of the ruling whether there are many sides to be lenient or not.`,
    '4#_': `(There Shach s.k. 40) Or one finds a case where they were rinsed by rainwater. Kereti challenged this text that Shach brought, that it deals where they were rinsed in rainwater that are not mekarev. But their rinsing pleases him and post-intent watering is mekarev. And one may say Shach holds like Tosafot Bava Batra (daf 97) that rainwater is not called watering by default until one intends them for watering and they do not help what pleases him. And see Chullin (daf 87). And see in Tel Melach and Chiddushei HaRamban. And it seems to me like what I wrote. And one may say it deals where the owners were wicked and there is no intent without action. However, the view of Tosafot and Chiddushei HaRamban is that they do not require specifically the owners' intent, for another's intent is also mekarev, unlike the Rambam.`,
    '5#_': `(Seif 10) A gentile serving in a Jewish home, etc. Shach wrote in the name of Pe'er HaAdam that regarding his slave and maidservant fear applies; it seems that if he is not his slave, fear does not apply since he is not his slave. And so too Pe'er HaAdam wrote explicitly. And it is not clear, for it is evident plainly in the Gemara in the first chapter of Chullin regarding inspection of the knife for Kutim, and in Avodah Zarah regarding "the guard need not sit and watch" but even though he goes out and comes in it is permitted — that even for all gentiles who are not his slaves, fear applies when going out and coming in. And so it seems from the language of Hagahot Maimoniot in Hilchot Maachalot Assurot chapter 6 halacha 10. And in the laws of Tefillin it is brought in Orach Chaim siman 32 seif 10. And so in Mordechai, chapter Kol HaBasar siman 1266. And so I found explicitly in Shach siman 118, and he rejected the words of Ohr HaChaim who explained that it speaks only of his slave. And may God set me on the path of truth, for before me came sixty copies of Lechem HaPanim and I saw that he copied Shach's words and added at the end the word "forever"; and he meant that for his slave and maidservant that he acquired, fear applies forever even without going out and coming in; but Shach never intended at all to say that for one who is not his slave fear does not apply when going out and coming in. And this is clear. And what Turei Zahav wrote — a gentile serving in a Jewish home, etc. — it seems at first glance that if he is not serving, behold it is forbidden in any case because of bishul akum. But for his slave and maidservant he is not forbidden according to one view that the Mechaber brought siman 113 seif 4. However, it is strained to explain thus the words of Tur, for he did not distinguish between his slave and maidservant and other gentiles. Also the words of the Mechaber are somewhat strained, for he brought two views in this siman 113. And one must say that therefore he wrote "a gentile serving in a Jewish home": if he is not serving, presumably he does not know the custom of Israel and fear does not apply, as the hagahah wrote. And what there is to infer — behold in any case it is forbidden because of bishul akum — it seems to me it deals where it was not yet cooked in a gentile's hand like ben drusai, and then stirring by a Jew helps for all; unlike if it was cooked in a gentile's hand like ben drusai, where stirring by a Jew does not help again according to Rashba that Tur brings there. And so in Shulchan Aruch there seif 9 — see there. And this is that they inferred Turei Zahav and Shach and wrote "and there the meat is in the pot," meaning it was not yet cooked at all, even like ben drusai, only that he placed the meat in the pot and it is forbidden because it was cooked without final rinsing. For even in kli sheni the hagahah in seif 9 forbids. And even boiling did not cook it in this like ben drusai, as is clear in Ashiri, chapter Kirah siman 11 — see there. And requires further study in siman 17. And it seems to me that in this the words of Beit Yosef are correct, who wrote that here it is only a d'rabbanan matter of blood that was salted, and Darkei Moshe wondered why he needed to say it is blood that was salted, for behold it is cooked before us — see there. And according to what I wrote, one must say it deals here where it was not yet cooked like ben drusai, for otherwise it would be forbidden because of bishul akum. And since it was not cooked like ben drusai, this is not blood in cooking, for blood in cooking is not such unless it was cooked like ben drusai. And proof for the matter from chapter Kol HaBasar (daf 112) regarding Bei Dugi, who challenged there "perhaps the lower reaches the upper" — what is he challenging, behold it is a d'rabbanan safek? Rather it is forced that if it did not reach, meaning it was not cooked like ben drusai, it is forbidden d'oraisa and it is a d'oraisa safek lechumra. And one may reject per what Chavot Da'at wrote, that even when its cooking rises we repair and extract from any safek, even d'rabbanan, as Mahari Abohav wrote in siman 10 and other Acharonim. However, for one who does not know the repair, it is not for him to forbid from safek. And so it is clear in Tosafot chapter Keitzad Tzolin (daf 111) s.v. dam, that it is d'oraisa. And so in responsum Beit Yaakov siman 172. And this is not like Ohr HaChaim general rule 6 siman 8, who wrote that before one-third roasting there is only d'rabbanan. At least according to Turei Zahav it must deal here where it was not cooked in a gentile's hand like ben drusai. However, the hagahah there seif 9 ruled that for his slave and maidservant he is not forbidden because of bishul akum, and that stirring by a Jew helps even after a gentile cooked it like ben drusai. And it is possible that although the meat was not yet cooked like ben drusai, the fatliquor of blood and salt was cooked. And as Rosh wrote, chapter Kirah siman 17.`,
    '6#_': `(There in the hagahah) And in one of them it suffices, or in speaking offhand, etc. Or going out and coming in, for he is afraid. And it is obvious that for a mumar speaking offhand does not apply when he knows that issur and heter depend on this, unless one finds cases as Taz and Shach wrote s.k. 42 — see there. And it seems to me that fear also does not apply to a mumar, for he supposes they hold him as a full Jew and will not check after him, as Tosafot and Rosh and Shach wrote at the beginning of the first chapter of Chullin (daf 3) regarding knife inspection for a mumar. And here is a kal v'chomer from there, for there it is a d'oraisa issur and nevertheless the mumar supposes they will not check after him because they hold him as a Jew even for a d'oraisa issur; all the more here, where it is only a d'rabbanan issur as I wrote, that it is blood that was salted — see there.`,
    '7#_': `(There Taz s.k. 24) And an incident came before us with a woman who cooked kosher meat and forgot whether she salted it first or not, and we permitted it because it is only a d'rabbanan safek lekula. And he said we establish it on its presumption that initially it was not salted, etc., for there is a majority against the presumption that most times they salt first, etc. Shaarei HaMelech, laws of mikvaot general rule 7, challenged Taz according to what Tosafot wrote in Pesachim (daf 9) in the name of R' Yochanan regarding a chaver who died and left produce — since it is certainly treifah d'rabbanan, a safek whether he tithed removes certainty. And here too, since this piece was certainly not salted, a safek does not remove certainty. And to reconcile: treifah produce were all in a presumption of issur status, unlike here regarding meat that was not salted. And in such a case Taz wrote siman 127 that it is not called itchezk issur when the issur is not on the whole piece but the issur was not clarified from the heter. And Shaarei HaMelech himself proved there in general rules 1 and 2 regarding itchezk issura in d'rabbanan, such as one in one that mixed — if the issur was recognizable from the heter, it is not called itchezk issura to forbid as a d'rabbanan safek born afterward, such as one was lost and it is unknown which. And so here, since it is not called itchezk issura of blood on the meat piece, we are lenient in the safek. And why rov was needed here is because the safek is in an action. And so in Taz, Orach Chaim siman 49, who gave as reason that safek in action is weaker. And what Binet Adam challenged on Taz, that he considered rov of those who salt — behold that is rov dependent on action — say that in d'rabbanan it helps, and Maharalach has support for this from Pesachim (daf 4) regarding one who rents a house on the fourteenth, that we hold the bed is in a presumption of having been checked, and this presumption is from the majority of people who check at that time as Maharalach wrote; and it is also rov dependent on action, and also itchezk issura of chametz in the house. And apply this here. And if one says here is a flaw — how did she forget a great action of salting and the like — say on the other side, how did a safek arise whether she salted; what brought it to this safek? Say she did a great action; but go learn from safek whether he prayed and certainty that he did not pray — he does not return to pray because prayer is d'rabbanan, and although he is certainly obligated to pray, if there is safek whether he prayed, how does certainty remove safek? Also how did he forget such a great action whether he prayed — it requires favor of heart, and do they speak of the feeble-hearted outwardly? All the more one is liable to remember salting, which hands are busy with all day. And if you say prayer is different because if we are lenient he may bless in vain — there is stringency as leniency, for here there is no blessing until we say there is concern for blessing in vain, and one may challenge one explanation in the Gemara Pesachim, etc., that even in d'rabbanan we do not say safek removes certainty; if so, why exempt from prayer and Kriat Shema for one who holds Kriat Shema is d'rabbanan — safek in reading, certainty he did not read, he does not return to read because it is d'rabbanan safek? And here there is no blessing until we say so. And strain to say that when they enacted d'rabbanan like prayer and Kriat Shema they are different, they enacted for certainty and not for safek; in any case we derive in such a case safek removes certainty, and all the more according to Tosafot there, that this "his animal, etc." is all d'oraisa, but d'rabbanan nevertheless he is obligated in tithes and it is certainly so, and the Gemara concedes that in d'rabbanan safek removes certainty. And Taz's words are clear, and he did not remove something improper from his hand; but still in its place as Shach wrote, what is the alarm — shall we take a kafila to taste whether there is taste of blood, certainly she forgot, and if not, certainly she did not forget; and because we are stringent not to rely on kafila in this matter we are lenient in the deed? And one must say as I wrote, that every piece is full of blood but they did not forbid except blood smeared on top, and therefore even if the taster tastes taste of blood there is no proof it was not salted as I wrote. And according to what I wrote, that this is in the category of safek removes certainty, the question is not difficult, for he can clarify by asking her mouth; in any case one need not clarify and need not taste kafila. And so in Chullin daf 97, the Gemara challenged: if so, if it fell into another pot it should not be forbidden; and Tosafot explained: even though it is safek whether all exited in the first pot or only part, it is d'rabbanan safek lekula. And one should understand: in falling into another pot that the Gemara forbids, he tasted kafila and therefore what d'rabbanan safek is here — tasting kafila would decide whether all exited in the first or not. And one must say the Gemara's question is where there is no kafila, and if we rely on d'rabbanan safek; if so, even for us who do not rely on kafila it is as if there is no kafila and we rely on d'rabbanan safek, and requires further study to be lenient, for one may reject that the Gemara's question is if the pot increased now and there is no taste of milk in it, nevertheless it is forbidden since initially there was less than sixty and nevela, and forbidden; unlike if safek d'rabbanan lekula, then it was never forbidden. But regarding tasting, in truth we do not rely — if it were possible to taste. Therefore requires further study. Tzitz Eliezer brought in Kereti: if safek whether milk was salted with meat or not, it is d'oraisa safek for it is species in a different species and forbidden; and so agreed Pri Chadash. And I say: if he peels the meat it is permitted, for milk spreads in salting per most poskim it does not spread and peel suffices; and see Turei Chayim general rule 38, and below siman 105 in Shach s.k. 33, that many authorities hold it does not spread more than a peel, and even those who are stringent did not decide; and certainly to spread until it stops, even in sixty, requires removal; and see there siman 105. And if so, there is double safek: perhaps there was no contact of its milk at all, and if you say there was contact, perhaps it does not spread more than a peel; and if you do not know which place touched until the place of peel is unknown, one in two is nullified as known, and see siman 92 in Shach. And what I wrote in Peleti, do not doubt at all. However, as Shach wrote, milk in meat is species in a different species and can be clarified by a gentile's tasting — how do we say lekula that we do not rely on him? And it seems from poskim' words that salting taste is weak and impossible to taste; and see Rambam Hilchot Maachalot Assurot chapter 15 halacha 34 — salted nevelah meat mixed with slaughtered meat is forbidden because the salt became nevelah, etc., and impossible to stand on taste or issur there — requires further study. Tzitz Eliezer wrote: if safek milk safek fat, it is species in species and nullified d'oraisa and is only d'rabbanan safek lekula; and Pri Chadash questioned him that there are two safekot to be stringent, perhaps they were salted together and perhaps the milk was salted and not the fat, etc. I did not understand his words: in the end there is safek whether the milk was salted or not — if it was not salted, permitted, and it is d'rabbanan safek — what difference whether two safekot or not; why should we be stringent in d'rabbanan issur?`,
    '8#_': `(There seif 13) And there they soaked it in water within three days, etc. Taz and the Acharonim wrote one should soak it well; but passing water over it does not suffice unless he pierced the meat for blood grooves — then in b'dieved one relies on this, etc.; nevertheless afterward needs another rinse before salting, even b'dieved, etc. And it seems this applies also to "soaked it well" within the three days; nevertheless afterward needs another rinse before salting even b'dieved, for it already dried. And Taz and Shach wrote that if it was not yet cooked, he rinses and salts again; and so in Mei Yisrael general rule 7 s.k. 1. And what Shach wrote "suffices b'dieved" means even if not yet cooked; and as Shach himself wrote s.k. 14. However, Minchat Yisrael on Turei Chayim general rule 5 siman 3 and general rule 15 siman 12 challenged Shach's words and wrote that permitting b'dieved with slight rinsing is only after cooking, but if not cooked, only salted, since there is repair in rinsing and salting again it is not called b'dieved — see there. And this is that they disagree with what the hagahah wrote below seif 2, that if he did not rinse only a little before salting it is permitted b'dieved even before cooking or after cooking, and there in note 48 for law — see there.`,
    '9#_': `(There seif 15) Unless there is sixty in the water against it. It seems if it spilled and it is unknown whether there was sixty against the meat, it is permitted, for this issur of kevush cannot be d'oraisa because blood is mixed in the water and "it shall be offered on the altar" does not apply, and it is like blood that was salted as Ohr HaChaim wrote general rule 6 din 20.`,
    '10#_': `(There seif 16) And if he salted in an unperforated vessel, etc. And in the hagahah: and if it is a moist thing we require sixty against the peel from the bowl. Shach concludes that even if the bowl is also hot, sixty against its peel suffices, for the unperforated vessel was not forbidden entirely, only a peel's depth. And as the hagahah wrote siman 98 seif 4 — see there. And in my humble opinion, for earthenware one must estimate against the whole if it is hot, ta'tei gavar — see siman 91 and siman 105, for earthenware absorbs much as Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Hagahot Maimoniot chapter 6 regarding unperforated vessel — permitted in other vessels and forbidden in earthenware, and so the Acharonim as Mei Yisrael wrote general rule 11 s.k. 5 (see there). And so it is clear explicitly in Darkei Moshe siman 98, who wrote in the name of Ohr HaChaim in an incident where an unperforated earthenware vessel fell into a pot and he ruled to estimate against the whole and not suffice with a peel alone — see there. And all the more in earthenware that is not unperforated. And in my humble opinion what Rashba wrote in the name of Raavad and Beit Yosef brings siman 98, that in an unperforated vessel one must estimate against the whole — deals with earthenware. For it is impossible to say it deals with other vessels, for Rashba himself wrote in the name of Raavad and Beit Yosef brings siman 98: that which we say in the Gemara Perek Gid HaNashe (daf 97) regarding chelev in its entirety we estimate — if by what exits from it in a vessel we know how much exited, this is specifically in chelev or earthenware where one cannot separate the issur from it; but not in metal vessels and the like where one can separate the issur from it, we estimate well how much exited from it. And from this those who disagree with him hold that even in earthenware it does not absorb more than a peel's depth. And so it is clear in Rambam chapter 6 of Hilchot Maachalot Assurot, who wrote: a bowl in which one salted meat, even if it was a thick limb, is forbidden to eat hot in it forever, for blood was already absorbed in the pottery — see there. Behold he wrote blood was absorbed in the pottery, implying in the whole; otherwise behold the limb itself is only a small amount like a peel's depth, and examine. And since the view of Rambam and Raavad and Ohr HaChaim and Rashal is to forbid the whole earthenware vessel, one may not be lenient. And not like Chiddushei HaRamban siman 70. However, in Mei Yisrael general rule 80 s.k. 11 he decided to be lenient. And in responsum Mekom Shemuel he agreed — see there. And so Ohr HaChaim wrote general rule 24 din 9. And see what I wrote in our words siman 98 s.k. 5, that therefore the hagahah there wrote that through heated salting it is not absorbed in the vessel only a peel's depth; and if it was cooked in it, one need not estimate, only against the peel — meaning specifically the first time; but since it was cooked in it once without sixty, presumably what was in a peel's depth went and was absorbed in the whole and the permitted absorbed in it became nevela, as stated there seif 5 and siman 92 regarding a drop that fell, etc. — see there. And siman 99 seif 2. And if so, here too if the bowl was heated, one must estimate afterward against the whole unless in a place of great loss. And requires further study on Shach's words, for it seems the bowl was heated empty or without sixty, for he wrote presumably there is no sixty; if so, one must estimate the whole. And it is possible it deals where they did not use it for anything beforehand, and so in Pri Megadim siman 98 he left in requires further study. And see in Mei Yisrael, who wrote that bli o bli does not make bli nevela.`,
    '11#_': `(Seif 16 in the hagahah) We require sixty against the peel from the bowl. See in Beit Avraham that in our bowl presumably there is sixty against the peel.`,
    '12#_': `(There in hagahah gimel) But it is permitted to return and salt meat in it. Or even without perforation if the meat already remained in its salting period and was rinsed — the reason is there is no melicha for vessels, for a vessel does not expel through salt. And what is written here at length is a scribal error, for for the length we do not say there is no melicha for vessels without perforation, as Darkei Moshe wrote siman 105 — see there. And one must say Hagahot Sh"D [and requires further study in Shach s.k. 83; and one may say that in other issurim it is different as Taz wrote siman 10]. And Shach wrote that even in earthenware we say thus, that there is no melicha for vessels. And to me requires further study, for it is possible that specifically in a perforated vessel there is no difference between earthenware and other vessels; but without perforation it seems this is in other vessels but not in earthenware, for Tosafot and Rosh chapter Keitzad Tzolin (daf 113) and Mordechai chapter Kol HaShabbat and Hagahot Sh"D wrote there is no melicha for wood. In truth Mordechai in chapter Kol HaBasar wrote in the name of Rabbenu Ephraim that there is no melicha for wood, metal, earthenware, and stone. And so in Rashba's responsum that Beit Yosef brings siman 70 and siman 105 and siman 137. And so in Shulchan Aruch there seif 4 regarding absorbed vessels of yayin nesech that one may return and salt meat in them — see there, and requires further study. However, even if we say thus, in earthenware as well there is no melicha for vessels; nevertheless it is no worse than cold, for we say in chapter Kol HaShabbat (daf 30): all vessels in which chametz was used cold, one may use for matzah cold. And many commentators explained: specifically chametz was used cold, but if chametz was used hot, forbidden to use for matzah even cold. And this applies to earthenware vessels. And there are two reasons: because earthenware vessels absorb much and expel even when cold, and therefore every cold use requires rinsing. And as Rashba wrote in the name of Rif and Ramban and so the Rav the Maggid chapter 5 of Hilchot Chametz, and so Rosh chapter Keitzad Tzolin, and so in my humble opinion the view of all of them, and Raavad, and Darkei Moshe brings siman 121, who wrote: earthenware bowls in which issur was used hot are forbidden to use even cold for moist things, because it is impossible to rinse them. And see in our words siman 91. And therefore, since it expels its expulsion into the salted meat, and melicha is like rotev for it, the meat is forbidden and rinsing does not help, as stated siman 10 regarding a knife — there one needs a peel. And in my humble opinion what the hagahah wrote, that it is permitted to salt in it meat already salted and rinsed, because there is no melicha for vessels — deals with other vessels and not earthenware. For who will be lenient against Rif and Ramban and Rashba and Hagahot Maimoniot and Rosh and all of them and Raavad, that even b'dieved one should forbid a peel's depth. And there is another reason: since it has no repair through hagalah, we are concerned lest he use it hot. And even for passing use it is forbidden. And so Mei Yisrael wrote general rule 11 s.k. 6 — see there, and my intention is according to his view, with God's help. And see there s.k. 11. And what Shach and Pri Chadash wrote, that presumably there is no sixty in the bowl against the peel — so in my humble opinion it is clear in the Gemara chapter Kol HaBasar (daf 111), that a bowl in which one salted meat is forbidden to eat hot in it. And he concludes that it is impossible to taste — Rashi: because it is forbidden, therefore it has no heter. Rif to Rava: and let him taste with an Aramaean kafila — did we not learn, etc.? He said: when I speak of forbidden, where there is no kafila. And Rif challenged well: why did he decide to say there is no heter for this hot when there is no kafila — behold with kafila one could permit? And from this we learn that by default there is no sixty against what was forbidden from the bowl, namely a peel's depth. For by law of the Gemara certainly melicha does not forbid more, as Darkei Moshe wrote siman 105 and Shach. And earthenware is worse. And the law of kafila tasting — see siman 98.`,
    '13#_': `(There seif 18 in the hagahah) But the other pieces on it are permitted. So too Ohr HaChaim, and it is explained there that even a peel is not required; and so wrote Taz. And in my humble opinion it is impossible to permit them without a peel. And clear proof from the Gemara chapter Kol HaBasar (daf 112) regarding fish and birds salted one with another — forbidden; and so Taz siman 70. And the Gemara challenged there: how is it? If in an unperforated vessel, why fish and birds — even birds with birds should be forbidden. And now what is the question — for these birds above do not even require a peel? And these fish and birds — even the fish above require a peel, as Shach wrote siman 70 s.k. 4 and the Acharonim there and Shach. Rather it is forced that birds with birds is also so. However, Mei Yisrael left it in requires further study whether birds above require a peel — see there in general rule 2 s.k. 22.`,
    '14#_': `(There seif 20 in the hagahah) And even before the salting period the issur is not because of the knife but because visible blood exited; and if he wishes he may rinse there and return to salt there. From this it is proven that if he cut a piece in two before salting after rinsing, he need not return to soak it, only rinse the place of the cut, as I wrote s.k. 1; and so explicitly in Bedek HaBayit and Lechem HaPanim. And do not challenge: behold it is called visible blood and therefore since blood came on the meat the meat should be forbidden, for we hold end of siman 70 that if visible blood fell on meat during its salting period it is immediately forbidden and there is no measure to the matter. And one may say it is not called visible blood on the absorbed blood in the piece, only for the matter that he must rinse it, for if he salts on it the salt will become full from it and will not expel blood, as Mordechai wrote in the reason for initial rinsing; but for this matter it is not called visible. Rather absorbed wet blood that exits afterward through salting, as I wrote, and examine. And see what I wrote in our words siman 10 s.k. 4.`,
    '15#_': `(There seif 21) In a place where salt is not found, they roast the meat until half its roasting and afterward cook it — thus Tur. And Beit Yosef showed its source from Hagahot Ashiri. And this is not that Tur did not bring Hagahot anywhere; but in truth it was hidden from Beit Yosef the words of Rosh, chapter HaRoeh siman 7, and Rashba in Taharat HaAdam, who wrote: and they sent from the yeshiva in a place that has no salt to roast it on fire. And after it reaches half, if he wishes to cook it in a pot, it is fine — see there. Behold it is clear explicitly as Tur wrote, and he did not need to make a weak inference from Hagahot as Beit Yosef wrote.`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `One must rinse. Be'er Heitev; and see in Beit Avraham siman 29 regarding visible blood that fell on meat and dried and hardened — rinsing and washing alone do not suffice, rather one must rub well — see there. And according to the reason, because of blood that dried on its surface, therefore here too one should be stringent and rub well at the time of rinsing to remove what hardened on the surface, unless it is still hot — then passing rinsing suffices entirely; nevertheless one should clean by hand, since sometimes it does not entirely slide off the meat through mere rinsing and moisture of visible blood remains on the surface even after rinsing; and so a person should teach in his home to be careful in this. And Radbaz part 2 siman 37 wrote: one who has no water to rinse meat has no repair to eat the meat through cooking in a pot, only through roasting on coals or on a spit and peeling the outer layer and eating the rest; and if it is close to slaughter and has not yet hardened and dried, it suffices if he wipes well so no blood remains on the surface and it is permitted without peeling — see there. And see below siman 76 that Shach's view is that for roasting he does not require initial rinsing at all, and see there in the name of Knesset HaGedolah that one should require initial rinsing for roasting — see there. And if he has only fruit juice, it will be explained below. And see Beit Yosef that in cold regions one should be careful not to rinse in very cold water lest it congeal — only place it near fire until the chill departs — see there.`,
    '2#_': `Meat that was full of ice. Be'er Heitev; and see in Degel Machaneh Ephraim, who wrote: if meat was salted while still frozen, if it was already rinsed or it is after twelve hours, it has no further repair through salting — only roast it; and if it is within twelve hours and not yet rinsed, rinse it only a little to remove the first salt, and thereby the expulsion holes are not sealed; and I doubt according to Ran's reason that initial rinsing is so the meat softens. And certainly rinsing while still frozen does no action to soften the meat, and if so he must rinse well now and the expulsion holes will seal and again there is no repair except roasting. However, regarding the law itself that rinsing seals expulsion holes, Taz disagrees — see there, and it seems his view is to be lenient in great loss; and so it seems that since because of freezing it did not expel at all, the holes were not sealed. And see in Mei Yisrael general rule 6 s.k. 14 and in Mishbetzot here and in Pri Megadim siman 70 s.k. 14, who brought later authorities' view that since there is repair through roasting, great loss is not a universal rule, for we find cases such as fat that froze where roasting is impossible; and see there s.k. 53 that if the meat was full of ice and melted within three days, forbid even b'dieved; and see in Divrei Shalom siman 3 regarding frozen from the outset — permit only roasting, and see there s.k. 53.`,
    '3#_': `And even if they soaked it. Be'er Heitev; and in responsum Maaseh Moshe siman 80, meat soaked in seawater in a ship in an unperforated vessel — since the water was salty and unfit to drink because of their saltiness, it is forbidden; and Machatzit HaShekel disagreed and wrote several reasons to permit, that water has no power even though salty to expel like salt itself; and even if it is double safek d'rabbanan lekula, for blood that was salted is d'rabbanan; also sea water unfit for drinking is not because of saltiness but because they are bitter. And see Radbaz's responsum regarding netilat yadayim, that sea water when heated on fire diminishes in bitterness and becomes fit to drink, etc. — see there, and it seems he deals where it was already given initial rinse in other water, only he is concerned lest it is like unsalted in unperforated vessel; and in his view to permit for reasons he wrote there. However, if not yet rinsed, there is concern because of visible blood on it. And in my responsa Beit Aleph part 2, which is in manuscript with me, I wrote regarding spring water from salt wells that they draw and heat and make salt, which are very salty and impossible to drink because of saltiness, and residents there sometimes use such water instead of salt and took such water and soaked meat before salting instead of initial rinsing, and wrote there that although one may say such water is sharp like brine, nevertheless one may say they have no power to spread from place to place like other salty brine; and even if it soaked kevush period, one may say there is no power in the blood mixture to become entangled through the water's medium and return to be absorbed through salting and roasting; nevertheless concern for visible blood on it. And I ruled to permit without kevush period, and if it remained kevush period forbid without great loss, and in great loss permit for roasting as below siman 70 in the name of Noda BiYehudah regarding meat that fell into brine unsalted — see there.`,
    '4#_': `However, in a place of great loss. And in Har HaKarmel siman 15 regarding great loss: there is no fixed amount, everything according to the eyes of the posek and according to time and circumstance and the person. And see in Sulam LaMinchah general rule 38, who brought in the name of Turei Daat siman 204 to distinguish between a barrel and a flask, brought below siman 126; and he also brought the words of Nishmat Shabbos siman 55 copied in Lechem HaPanim, who wrote that loss in principal more than a sixth is great loss even for a rich man. And in Beit Hadassah he wrote that great loss is a gold and a half polish, close to this measure as I heard in the name of the Maggid Rabbi Shmuel, may his memory be blessed; and the essence is there is no measure to the matter, only everything according to the posek's mind and according to time and place — the rich according to his wealth, etc., as written in Yoreh Deah siman 248 regarding measure of charity taken from women; and also according to the matter of the ruling whether there are many sides to leniency or not — all one matter, one who increases and one who decreases, provided he directs his heart — see there. And see in my Beit Aleph on treifot siman 39 what I wrote on this matter.`,
    '5#_': `And specifically if it was already cooked. Be'er Heitev, that Shach brought and in s.k. 96 the adder added from Taz, and they are words contradicting each other; and what he wrote in s.k. 96 that Taz is lenient — see in the Acharonim. And in Rav Meshulam siman 15 in Likutei HaGaon, the author of Chavatzalet HaSharon wrote he saw written in the name of the Gaon Rabbi Moshe of Radin, may his memory be blessed: a piece of meat salted on one side is permitted only if the salted side is on top, but if below — forbidden, for blood does not spread upward — and this is an error; and between going and coming it was switched for him, for certainly salt draws blood and pulls it to the place where it is, whether from below to above or the reverse; and the sense testifies, but regarding spreading, meaning to convey from it onward, blood has no power to go from heat of salting from below to above, only to move from above to below — see there, and it is clear the listener heard and erred and hung empty words on the Gaon Rabbi Moshe, may his memory be blessed; and we merited to discuss it in Sefer Tiferet Moshe, and the viewer will see he never had such a thing in mind; his words refer to what is said here that after beforehand there is no repair in second salting, for the unsalted side will absorb — Taz wrote there is repair in salting again and the salted side should be below so it does not spread upward, only he must know for certain it did not flip, for by default it tends to flip — see there, what he wrote regarding intestines salted: there is repair to return and salt from inside, and we are not concerned the inner side will absorb since there is no brine, therefore there one does not need even l'chatchila to salt only the outer side alone — see there. And in Shiyurei Berachah s.k. 23 he stood on this and wrote it depends on the dispute of Shach and Mei Yisrael regarding the reason of spreading a peel's depth — see there. And in my humble opinion, in such a case where it is unknown, one may be lenient because of the peel, only Shulchan Aruch does not deal with repair such as needing to stand over it lest it flip at all; nevertheless if it is great loss, one may do so with care not to flip, since otherwise the Mechaber's view is to be lenient regarding salted on one side.`,
    '6#_': `Approximately one-third of an hour. Be'er Heitev; and see the words of R' Alexander Ziskind in chapter Shabbat in Pesachim, that what Taharat HaAdam brought from there as proof for salting period being one hour is no proof at all, for although Rosh wrote like roasting period, nevertheless roasting itself does not require an hour, only with its preparation we give an hour — see there; and see Be'er Yaakov; and see in Chavot Yair that regarding mil period in salting one needs like walking two-fifths of an hour, which is twenty-four minutes — see there. And it seems since the reason is to be stringent d'oraisa as written in Mevaser Shemesh according to Rambam and Raavad in Pesachim, and we hold blood in cooking is only d'rabbanan, we are lenient.`,
    '7#_': `One may rely b'dieved. Be'er Heitev; and what he wrote "and it was cooked" — because otherwise he salts again and in any case, nevertheless one may not salt it with other meat, as Shiyurei Berachah wrote and in Degel Machaneh Ephraim, that even if not cooked it is considered b'dieved since it was already rinsed, he must salt again and there is loss; therefore he wrote that even Shach is stringent only if it remained the mil period, and b'dieved if it remained more than half an hour one may be lenient if it was already rinsed that he need not return to salt it.`,
    '8#_': `Permitted to rinse meat in fruit juice. Be'er Heitev; and see Radbaz part 2 siman 37, that rinsing meat in all kinds of liquids, even not of the seven liquids. And see Machatzit HaShekel, that from Pri Chadash it is clear l'chatchila one should not rinse even final rinsing in fruit juice; and likewise as Kereti wrote; and in responsum Knesset HaGedolah he is stringent in final rinsing even b'dieved.`,
    '9#_': `For the water in the vessel. Be'er Heitev; Noda BiYehudah part 2 siman 41: meat still in its salting period that fell into water in which the meat soaked before salting, within the salting period — certainly permitted, not to speak of if there is no concern of unperforated vessel such as salting on a grate over wood as Shach wrote; but even if it fell on the grate's edges where water is inside, permitted because water nullifies the salt's power. However, even if it fell after the salting period, it seems to permit, and one whose heart struck him in any case should salt again even though it already expelled its blood, for since expulsion holes sealed, etc., and this is not so agreed; and see Tzitz Eliezer siman 8, and fit to rely on it in such a case; but he should be careful to salt alone and not with other meat. And see Beit Yosef s.v. an incident that was salted, etc., and in Pri Chadash end of siman 70 he permits too, and the posek may rely on this plainly — see there.`,
    '10#_': `Sixty against the salt. Be'er Heitev; and see in my responsa Beit Aleph siman 28, explanation of Taz's words s.k. 19, "and one should not practice thus," etc. — at first glance his words are astounding — see there.`,
    '11#_': `Even in kli sheni. Be'er Heitev; and see in Teshuvat Chen siman 11 regarding beef placed in kli rishon; and what Be'er Heitev wrote about Taz's incident — see in Mei Yisrael general rule 1 s.k. 16 and in Sulam LaMinchah and in Shiyurei Berachah part 2 siman 69, that his view is like Knesset HaGedolah; and so Bahag; and so appears the view of Sefer HaMelamed end of laws of mikvaot, who wrote this law is in dispute — Tosafot Pesachim daf 9 s.v. shetehei, etc. — see there; and so the view of Rabbi Yitzchak in his book Or Yekarot; and Machatzit HaShekel wrote that in great loss as Taz and Pri Chadash and Peleti one may rely; and so appears Pri Megadim's view; and in Pri Megadim part 1 siman 11 he wrote: a woman sent meat from her city to her husband in another village for Shabbat and did not write in the letter that the meat was salted, and they cooked the meat thus and again a safek arose whether it was salted or not, and some want to compare to what Taz wrote s.k. 24 regarding a woman who forgot, etc., and he rejected this and ruled one may not permit unless with compelling indications that she certainly salted, such as the messenger could only come close to Shabbat so there was no time to salt and cook on Shabbat; however in Shiyurei Berachah part 2 siman 69 he wrote regarding one sent meat on erev Shabbat and stored it in an oven and again remembered perhaps it was not salted, and wrote several reasons to forbid until he asks and clarifies it was salted, because the way is to send before salting so it looks nicer; similar to what we say at the beginning of Ketubot; but putting water on meat does not sell well, also no reason to think it is something that can stand through licking the meat; and although Rashal forbids licking, this is not the law everywhere; and also possible through kafila tasting or rinsing a little visible blood, etc.; and apart from law, how permit something clarifiable after Shabbat, etc.; and not like Pri Megadim, who sided to be lenient that the messenger, etc., is not compelling indication; it is not compelling, for if her intent was to salt, she would have time to salt and store near Shabbat or roast on a spit needing less waiting; and since it will become clear, do not be lenient even in double safek; and so it was in truth, after Shabbat it became clear it was not salted and the delay caused damage through him — and so it seems to me to rule — see there.`,
    '12#_': `To salt in it again. Be'er Heitev; and in Shiyurei Berachah part 3 siman 66: water in which meat was rinsed before salting that reddened and there was not sixty against the blood in them — let it dry and it is permitted to salt meat in it to expel its blood, but forbidden to put into forbidden food; and if it is very salty, do not leave it in the house lest they err to put from it into food, for although b'dieved it does not forbid, l'chatchila good to be careful — see there.`,
    '13#_': `And if it was cooked, sixty is required against it. Be'er Heitev; and see Turei Chayim general rule 2, who wrote thus; and although above he wrote regarding great loss it is permitted, nevertheless here plainly, and it is possible he holds here one should forbid that piece even in great loss, since its blood congealed inside it — properly one should be concerned lest cooking softened it somewhat so that through cooking it spread from place to place, and nevertheless it does not exit outside; and even the Mechaber concedes in this according to Taz's reason, what spread exited outside; even according to what I wrote below Taz's reason — requires further study. And the essence reason is as Rosh ruled, that spreading place to place in the piece itself is permitted; if so, here too one may permit in great loss. And so Pri Chadash, that for the Mechaber the piece itself is permitted; and see in s.k. 96.`,
    '14#_': `And one may not eat it cooked. Be'er Heitev; and in Shiyurei Berachah part 1 regarding goose fat that was not rinsed for three days, one may be lenient to roast through salting as its law; and in any case scrape the skins well from the meat stuck to the fat, also cut and rinse and salt them — see there, brought in Turei Meir siman 75. However, in Noda BiYehudah siman 24 he scolded the posek for permitting this, and wrote that if he ruled to permit the meat he is certainly intentional to feed forbidden matter, for even Shiyurei Berachah permits only the fat alone and not the meat, and there was no room to err; and they scold him and remove him from ruling further. However, even the posek who permits the fat alone should be scolded, and if the act was done and the fat was rendered and cooked, the vessels and all mixtures are forbidden, unlike Shiyurei Berachah, for although fat is not presumed with blood, nevertheless there is blood in it; and further, goose skin is not all fat, for even a thin goose has a skin membrane, and that membrane certainly has blood, and the Acharonim spoke only regarding fat in intestines that is only fat alone; and Shivat Tziyon wrote all poultry skin is fat — this is not so, etc. — see there; and see below siman 75 what I wrote there regarding Elazil salted on one side. And in responsum Or Ne'elam siman 19 regarding fat of a hen that remained three days without salting and rinsing, and the woman salted and rinsed and rendered the fat and made foods for Shabbat and also sold to others who made dishes for Shabbat, and it became known it remained three days — he wrote to permit the remainder of the dishes and vessels because of double safek: perhaps there was sixty in the dishes, for perhaps there is not so much blood in the dish; and also perhaps it exited through salting; and in any case not so much remained that it cannot be nullified; and this is not safek lacking knowledge that is safek for the whole generation; and also it is d'rabbanan safek as Taz wrote in safek whether she salted; and although Noda BiYehudah disagrees because itchezk issura, one may distinguish: there the safek is on the piece, unlike here where the fat dissolved in the pot. And it seems Noda BiYehudah may also concede here; and as is clear from his words regarding fat in intestines there is more reason to be lenient, for there is no skin; and perhaps in great loss one may permit the mixtures; and in any case one who relies to be lenient to permit the vessels after prior use certainly did not lose; and in Givat Shaul siman 2 regarding a fat goose that remained three days without salting and has no heter except roasting — forbidden to place a vessel under it to receive the grease; and b'dieved if they placed a vessel to receive the fat and it was already half roasted, permit b'dieved like cooking after roasting b'dieved; and although Beit Yosef gives reason of nullification in gravy, here perhaps there is no sixty against the blood that exits in the grease; nevertheless b'dieved one may rely on Beit Yosef's reason that all its blood exited through roasting and therefore permitted after cooking, and the same here. And see Machatzit HaShekel what he wrote on Bedek HaBayit and Pri Megadim and raised that regarding goose that remained three days one should forbid even in great loss; but if he did not give initial rinse, be lenient in such a case — see there; and see in the small seif after this.`,
    '15#_': `And after they roasted it, do not cook it. Be'er Heitev; and in Shemesh Tzedakah siman 37 regarding meat that remained three days and immediately about half an hour after the three days ended they tore it into many tears and rinsed it, and some soaked it like other meat soaking and salted it, and after salting and second rinsing the redness goes and whiteness comes — Heaven forbid to permit cooking it, even if they salted it many times and the blood is clean and white — do not pay attention to this — see there. And see there one should not rule heter to cook after roasting, and likewise not permit salting after roasting and soaking it many days in an unperforated vessel, for kevush is like cooking, and therefore do not leave it many days such as (through smoking), for we are concerned lest he forget and cook it — see there. And see there the reason forbidden to cut meat that remained three days fine fine and mix fat and spices in it to fill pastries from it to eat them roasted on coals or buried in hot coals wrapped in paper or without paper, for intestines are hard and do not hold and push it out and the meat inside becomes as if cooked in a vessel and forbidden; and also there is reason to decree lest he come to cook from them, for we forbid to leave it, etc. — see there; and see Machatzit HaShekel that in his letter to David his view is to permit in such a case, and wrote on it that Even HaEzer disagreed with him and forbade; and regarding the essential law he already ruled in Shemesh Tzedakah, and other great ones that one should not leave them — see there. And see in Teshuvat Chen siman 20, who extended on the words of Ohr HaChaim and the Mechaber and concluded one should not budge from Ohr HaChaim's ruling that without piercing three days, slight rinsing does not help; and if he did not soak at all, forbidden to cook, only roast; and if he cooked, be lenient b'dieved; and if he pierced within three days, even if he gave slight rinsing within three days, permitted to cook; and all this after it already remained; but l'chatchila there is a difference if it is pierced — proper that he soak it well; and if not pierced, forbidden l'chatchila even if he soaks many hours in water, since the sages of Austria forbid even b'dieved, we have no permission to be lenient l'chatchila — see there, who brought from Torat Yekutiel that his view is that even b'dieved one should not be lenient even if pierced, only through slight soaking; and when not pierced, even through slight soaking one should not be lenient; and he brought proofs and it is not compelled; one who is stringent should be stringent for himself and not for others — see there. And see there siman 21, who wrote that in such a case where it remained and he passed water over it in mere pouring, we are stringent; nevertheless in slight loss it is permitted to cook it after roasting — see there. And what Be'er Heitev wrote that Taz wrote in the name of Rashal on the meat, etc. — see in Noda BiYehudah, who challenged this; and what he wrote regarding salted with other meat, etc. — behold Taz wrote a posek permitted and it seems he erred, etc.; and behold I found his brother the Gaon Rabbi Yehudah HaLevi, may his memory be blessed, in his responsa, who permits because of kebol'o kach polto, etc.; and what Mei Yisrael wrote general rule 14, that according to what Rama ruled to be lenient b'dieved if he cooked after roasting, etc. — see there, explained so in the words of Rabbi Yehudah HaLevi — see there. And see in Chiddushei HaRamban that his view is to be lenient in these laws, that Taz wrote according to this being only stringency of the Geonim — see there. And see in my responsa Beit Aleph siman 27: meat that remained three days that was salted with poultry meat together, and after salting and rinsing they cooked them together in a barrel and salted them as usual for long preservation and it was kevush thus many days — forbid even what is outside the brine because of taste, etc. — see there regarding taste. And see Radbaz part 2 siman 138; and see Chiddushei HaRamban that one may permit fat that remained three days without salting, that after salting as its law one cooks the fat in water that has sixty, since in great loss we hold that piece is permitted; and pour the water and this is not nullifying issur; and further that the fat returning to absorb from the water is not nullification of issur, for absorption comes after nullification; and further that our intent is only to liquefy the fat somewhat like siman 84; but to mix it with other fat it seems forbidden — see there. And at first glance according to the latter reason, even to mix with other fat it would be permitted if it is not his own fat, for permitted; on the contrary, somewhat better, for water is not the way to liquefy fat thus, it appears intended to nullify issur, unlike other fat that is not his own, whose way is to liquefy all his fat together and it does not appear as nullifying issur. However, it seems without the second reason alone it suffices to permit, for there he does not add to it only the quantity it has because of the fat, and likewise in baking bread from wormy flour already nullified — only that it did not come to a practical ruling in this; we say his intent is not to nullify. And similarly here if kosher fat absorbed blood and it is known there is sixty in the fat against the blood, although taste still forbids, permitted to liquefy it, for the intent is only to liquefy and not to nullify; and so in other issurim that fell into sixty, permitted to cook afterward; but nevertheless, when less than sixty fell, that it would be permitted to add from outside and say his intent is not to nullify — we never heard this; and although blood that was salted is only d'rabbanan and also blood in cooking, nevertheless Rama already wrote siman 99 that even to add in d'rabbanan is forbidden. And this is clear: if we say that in such a case it is permitted l'chatchila to nullify, then according to Tur and Mechaber siman 92 the piece itself returns to being permitted — there would be heter l'chatchila to cook a piece that absorbed issur in water that has sixty against the issur, and we say since anyway the way to cook meat is not intent to nullify but to cook the meat; or one may say that although he does it as he was, he makes nullification through the addition, for through this addition he innovates the act of nullification — there is concern of ein mevatlin; and see Chullin daf 100. However, when he liquefies in water and pours the water, it seems this is not nullifying issur, for this is like hagalah that permits because the intent is only to expel the issur and not to nullify it; and see Taz, Orach Chaim siman 452, and Pri Chadash siman 182, that what was nullified through expulsion in hagalah we do not care, since he does not benefit from it; nevertheless requires further study, for granted that piece is permitted because of what Taz wrote regarding meat cooked without salting, that what spread exited outside; nevertheless it is obvious that also in that piece remains part of the taste of blood that spread proportionally, only it is permitted because it was nullified in sixty, and this is b'dieved, but l'chatchila it does not leave the category of ein mevatlin; and in this, absorption after nullification — only at the time of nullifying the fat it takes its share of blood and is nullified in it — see there in Chiddushei HaRamban in explanations, who wrote do not say that according to Taz's reason, because it did not spread, he gives reason that it is taste of blood that did not spread — see there. And this is regarding taste of blood that did not spread; but blood that spread, which is the piece of issur, the meat receives taste at the time of separation from it; except that it was squeezed to the pot and only less than sixty remains — permitted; and in any case l'chatchila it should have been forbidden (and see Chiddushei HaRamban, who wrote according to the Mechaber even if it fell into another pot in less than sixty it does not forbid, for once squeezed its taste was weakened and weak taste of permitted does not forbid, for there is no name of taste on the essence; but here in blood of limbs that we say even if it did not spread from its place in the first pot it will spread in the second pot, this reason does not apply, for it is substance and substance is not squeezed through sixty; and since it now spread and there is not sixty in it, forbidden; and see Mishbetzot, who forbids too). However, Taz's answer on the Mechaber is astounding, for if ordinary blood were absorbed in the piece and fell into sixty, the piece would remain in its issur, for we say what did not exit — see Taz siman 106; and what is better about this blood, and who says it did not spread from it — perhaps most blood in it spread or all of it and part exited and part did not; and to say the nature of cooking is that what is aroused to exit, all exits, therefore in ordinary blood or issur we say perhaps remains inside what was not aroused to exit at all through cooking — whence do we have this? All the more for Rashba, who holds even what expelled outside, weak kafila taste and there is no issur taste — nevertheless forbidden, for there remains in it slight taste from this blood that separated, taste of issur; and so in blood from elsewhere; and certainly so. If so, what is better about blood of the piece; therefore the Mechaber's view seems like Rosh, who holds spreading place to place within the piece is permitted for us; if so, here too one may permit the fat through cooking in water that has sixty and pour the water, not because of Chiddushei HaRamban's reason, for according to Taz one should not permit except in great loss — we rely on Rosh; and if so, there is no issur here, for he does not intend to nullify, only to expel blood from inside the piece into the water and pour the water like hagalah water; and if remains inside, even if it spread inside place to place, permitted according to Rosh; and one may rely in great loss, especially here where the fat was salted as its law and there is only stringency of Geonim and also blood that was salted or cooked is d'rabbanan; and the essence issur ein mevatlin l'chatchila — the poskim agreed it is d'rabbanan; if so, do not be stringent here; but one should not rule thus for everyone, only for one who knows to be careful still that there be sixty in the water against the fat and not use the water for eating needs, only pour them — so in my humble opinion; and it is a simple matter that the posek who permits heter here must consider he does not lose his reward through firewood expense, for when he wants to do work in much water to cook the fat and there will be sixty times its quantity, he will certainly need to waste much firewood, for the water will go to loss; and if after all the trouble and firewood expense only a slight amount remains of the fat's equivalence, obviously the posek should not permit doing so, for the entire basis of this heter is built on great loss, that in great loss one may rely on the Mechaber that in sixty the piece itself is permitted, unlike when here is only saving a little — obviously one should not do so, and we do not find this heter except in a place where wood and trouble are cheap and fat expensive.`,
    '16#_': `Less than half an hour. Be'er Heitev; and see Turei Meir s.k. 1 regarding soaking, and in Orach Chaim siman 321 I wrote in these laws — see there; and see in Dvar Shmuel siman 300, who was uncertain if permitted to return and extend the three days by another soaking, and wrote that since it is not mentioned in Shas one may be lenient in such safekot — see there, and brought briefly in Lechem Kodesesh — see there; and see in Sulam LaMinchah general rule 4, who wrote: and so I heard ruling of elder rabbis to soak meat specifically within three days like half an hour, but mere rinsing — not so, end of his words; and already practiced even l'chatchila through mere rinsing; and it seems one should not be lenient l'chatchila where possible through soaking half an hour, since he wishes to leave it another three days. And once it happened that a butcher took frozen meat from kitchens to his home and it thawed there and three days passed without soaking; however what thawed was within twelve hours, and the posek ruled the meat permitted, for after thawing from freezing and ice it is moist in the water of thawing and is like he rinsed through passing, which is permitted b'dieved; and I agreed with him if the butcher would say he sprinkled on its surface a little, because there was also great loss; nevertheless one should warn the butcher to soak it for half an hour before selling so it softens well, since sometimes the buyer has only an hour's urgency to cook and soaking in passing, and since this already passed three days, although we may rely on thaw water, nevertheless l'chatchila good to soften it now through soaking well — I shortened.`,
    '17#_': `Bloody meat. Be'er Heitev; and see in my responsa siman 28 what I wrote on Shach's words s.k. 15 on this matter.`,
    '18#_': `There is sixty in the water against it. Be'er Heitev; and in Shabbetai Yaakov siman 27: meat that was soaked in water twelve hours and afterward soaked in other water another hour — it does not combine, for it is not called kevush except twenty-four consecutive hours — see there; and see in responsum Keter Kehunah siman 36, who extended on this and concludes also to permit, and also in concern of d'oraisa issur the lenient one in non-great-loss did not lose — see there.`,
    '19#_': `And the vessel into which that brine fell. Be'er Heitev; and in responsum Pri Tevuah siman 23: salting brine that fell on a porcelain vessel at the vessel's rim — he wrote to permit for several reasons, since it fell outside, and also it is in a high place; when the hot items reach opposite the vessel's ear that they use it through pouring into it, then there will be sixty, etc., as a thing whose way is to use abundantly, etc.; and also because of irui it will not expel from the vessel's outer wall, and especially porcelain — some wrote it is somewhat like glass, etc.; and especially according to Rashi, brine after salting is considered cold, etc. (and what he wrote on Pri Megadim interpreting Shach's words that in every case the whole is forbidden — these are idle words; for Pri Megadim himself brings there Turei Chayim general rule 11 several times, etc. — see there, who brought from Ohr HaChaim who holds so). And see in Pri Megadim several laws on this; and see below siman 91.`,
  },
  peleti: {
    '1#_': `One must rinse, etc. — behold, in the body of the law, whether blood that was salted or roasted or cooked is forbidden d'oraisa or only d'rabbanan, most later poskim adopted the view of Tosafot and their supporters that it is only d'rabbanan. However, Rashi and Rambam hold d'oraisa, for in chapter Keitzad Tzolin Rashi explained: "the heart — we tear it, we do not tear it, he does not violate for bird heart, but for animal heart where there is an olive's bulk he violates even though it was already cooked"; and so in chapter Or VeRotav we say "he skimmed the blood and ate it" — Rashi explained "raw and cooked." And they wondered: behold we say in the Gemara Menachot daf 21a that blood in cooking and blood in salting one does not violate — therefore they ruled the opposite, that in truth one does not violate. And according to their words it is not understood to challenge Rashi, for Rashi follows his line that Rava sat and stated that law that blood in cooking one does not violate, and Abaye challenged from a baraita that teaches "he skimmed the blood and ate it — he is liable," and answers: here in heat that returns, here in raw that does not return; and the Gemara challenged: in raw, even though it returns, let us say since it was rejected, R' Yochanan said coagulated blood — since it was rejected, let it be rejected; and Rashi explained: he thought even in non-sacred animals (one should understand why he bothered to challenge from this baraita "he skimmed the blood and he is liable" on Rava and did not challenge on exempting coagulated blood even in non-sacred animals according to Rashi; and in the baraita he is liable for coagulated blood. And one may say exempt, for R' Yochanan cited karet, for we require blood fit for sprinkling of holy offerings, as Rashi wrote s.v. blood in cooking; and what we teach "he skimped and ate" is lashes, for there is no case where it would be less than blood of limbs that are not in the category of sprinkling and not for atonement and sprinkling, nevertheless they are under a negative commandment; and the same for coagulated blood; but to Ze'iri he challenged well, for he said one does not violate, implying there are no lashes either; and why he afterward challenged from R' Yochanan is not on the body of the law, only for proof that one should not distinguish between skimming in heat and skimming in cooking, for R' Yochanan exempts even coagulated though it returns, because once rejected it is rejected). And the Gemara answers: here in the inner sin-offering, here in the outer sin-offering — Rashi explained: what we teach "he skimmed the blood — he is liable" is in the outer sin-offering, and the same in non-sacred animals, for they are fit for atonement; and what Ze'iri said one does not violate is in the inner sin-offering; and on this the Gemara gives the reason for R' Chiya's view, who distinguishes between inner and outer, for in this it is written "and he shall take and place," and in this "and he shall dip and sprinkle and skim," etc., they are not fit for sprinkling; and Rava on his own says even in the inner sin-offering if one ate it he is liable, since corresponding to them it is fit in the outer sin-offering — perforce if so, the conclusion is Ze'iri who said one does not violate is according to R' Chiya who distinguishes inner from outer — he spoke of the matter in the inner, but not in the outer, and all the more in non-sacred animals; and for Rava who does not distinguish inner from outer, one is always liable, and all the more in non-sacred animals as we teach "he skimmed," etc., he is liable, and Ze'iri is not correct at all, and one is always liable. And see Tosefta printed in the Gemara, clear explicitly for Rashi's explanation, that Rava argues on Ze'iri who said one does not violate, and he is the later authority. Therefore, what is all the noise from the Acharonim and Pri Chadash among them on Rashi from this Gemara — for according to Rashi the matter is clear that in non-sacred animals all agree one violates, and Rava disagrees on the entire matter of Ze'iri, and there is no wonder on Rashi; and Tosafot who disagree with Rashi on this follow their line, for they challenged there in Tosefta on Rashi: how does Rava contradict things Abaye said in his name; and also what is the Gemara's difficulty, why need a verse, derive for me blood that was salted one does not violate; and what is the difficulty — for even if salted it does not immediately coagulate and remains clear, and therefore still fit for sprinkling and we read "and he shall sprinkle"; therefore they explained the distinction between inner and outer sin-offering is with clear and coagulated blood, but for blood that was salted or cooked, all agree it is disqualified. And in truth, for an understanding person the Tosafot's challenge on Rashi is easily resolved: what they challenged on the Gemara, why need a verse that one may not salt holy blood, derive blood that was salted — they challenged that to answer say it deals with clear blood, and this is not a difficulty, for Rashi himself explained blood that was salted in both non-sacred and holy — they are no longer fit; and although Rashi himself distinguishes sin-offerings that are eaten from those not eaten, implying Rashi holds this is all b'dieved, that he does not descend to distinguish between "and he shall take" and "and he shall sprinkle," and all are exempt, and if so the whole preceding sugya is according to him that he does not distinguish sin-offering from sin-offering and he did not hold the difference between "and he shall take" and "and he shall sprinkle," and if so it is obvious one should not distinguish coagulated from clear, and the Gemara challenged well why need a verse to exclude salting from blood; but for the conclusion that we have newly taught in the Gemara a difference between eaten and not eaten sin-offerings, for in this it is written "and he shall sprinkle," etc., it is obvious there is no place for the Gemara's challenge. And in truth, for the conclusion all the above is rejected, and it is obvious that for Rashi the Gemara's challenge why need a verse to exclude salting from blood does not apply to the conclusion, for it was needed for eaten sin-offerings; and perforce one must say it applies according to the view before the distinction was established; if so, Tosafot's challenge is resolved. And what they challenged, Rava on Rava, or that which Rava said is his own and his teacher's — one is his and one his teacher's, and it is common in Shas and Tosafot that we have the text Rava in place of Rabbah; if so, there is no challenge on Rashi. And there are clear indications for Rashi from that which we establish for him in tractate Keritut on bird heart that there is not an olive's bulk in its blood — it is strained to say therefore he spoke of raw heart not cooked; also proof from that which we derive in Pesachim and Nazir taste like essence from gentile cooking, and Tosafot challenged: whence for Nazir, perhaps for Nazir there is heter for its issur; and answer: even for Nazir the Torah commanded to kasher, and still ask: whence blood, perhaps there is heter for its issur if cooked; and needless to say Nazir proves, for also for Nazir there is heter for its issur, nevertheless forbidden as Tosafot wrote: what is Nazir to us that there is heter joining to issur from "his attendant" — unlike blood; and from this it is proven that for the conclusion blood in cooking is forbidden. Also for Rashi proof from that which we say Chullin daf 113b: to Shmuel, if so two kids remain, for Shmuel holds forbidden chelev and milk and nevelah come from one verse, blood too is not a kid, etc., and Tosafot challenged: what of it that blood is not a kid, on the contrary for this a kid is needed — see there. And behold, according to Rashi's explanation that issur chal al issur we derive in Bava Kama from this kid that Scripture includes and we learn from it chelev and nevelah, that from both of them issur applies — which do you exclude, for they are from the category of kid; and one may say thus the Gemara explains: blood too is not a kid, and if so issur chal al issur does not apply to them; and if you ask, to reconcile — but Tosafot follow their line that they hold blood in cooking is permitted d'oraisa, and if so there is no issur chal al issur in Torah, and needless as they challenged, and challenged well; therefore Rashi answered Tosafot's question, for he holds blood in cooking one violates; and if you ask, also Rambam's view is like Rashi, and so Rif, who copied this statement on "tear the heart," dealing with bird heart; and they wondered what difference for law — he copied it only to teach that animal heart one violates, for blood in cooking is forbidden d'oraisa, and therefore he did not copy anywhere that blood in cooking is d'rabbanan; and so Rosh did not copy anywhere that blood in cooking is d'rabbanan; and so Rosh, from his words in several places he is uncertain regarding blood in cooking, and Mahari Abohav copied Rambam's language in Bava Kama: one does not violate because of Bava Kama, implying one violates because of blood; therefore the Acharonim adopted this as primary, that blood in cooking is only d'rabbanan issur — for law requires further study.`,
    '5#_': `If he cut — Pri Chadash challenged these things Mordechai wrote: through knife pressure it expels, but he and Tosafot and many authors hold specifically with heat of the slaughterhouse we say so, not knife pressure alone. And one may say the essence there is to absorb and expel inside, and from a hard thing like a knife; but to expel and absorb only regarding soft meat we say even without heat of the slaughterhouse it absorbs, and do not challenge this view that holds when he cuts he must return to rinse because of knife expulsion — if so how did the Gemara say the butcher needs two knives, one to cut meat and one to cut chelev, for with one knife we fear he cuts chelev first; and Ran wrote it deals where they slaughtered in the slaughterhouse, so no further rinse is needed — otherwise what of it that he cut chelev first, in any case he needs initial rinse and wipes the chelev; and according to this view that when he cuts he always needs initial rinse, Ran's question on the Gemara is difficult, that always if he cuts he needs rinse — what is there to fear, and one must say these authorities hold like those who hold below siman 76 that roasting does not require initial rinse, for fire draws even visible blood — see below siman 66; and if so one should fear he cuts chelev first and afterward cuts meat with it and chelev smears on it and afterward he roasts the meat and there is no rinsing at all — examine.`,
    '6#א': `And on this one may rely b'dieved; and Pri Chadash wrote this means after it was already cooked, but if not cooked he salts again, even though it was rinsed we do not say the expulsion holes sealed, for it did not remain the salting period and they are not sealed — and he challenged: perhaps the law is like those authorities who do not require an hour, and he strained to reconcile, and his words are astounding — what strictness is here, behold on this we judge, and all the safek is whether this small period suffices to expel the blood or not; if so in any case if it sufficed, behold all blood expelled and no concern and permitted; and if we fear the time did not suffice and the piece still absorbs blood, if so the expulsion holes also are not yet sealed and even salting again helps — and his words were written without examination hastily.`,
    '6#ב': `An hour — the Rav, author of Taharat HaAdam, brought this law from that which we say in chapter Makom SheNahagu: on erev Pesach they advance the daily offering an hour so one hour remains free for roasting the Pesach — learn that roasting measure is an hour, and also for salting we estimate like roasting one hour, namely an average hour, for necessarily in Jerusalem day and night were equal and this hour is an average hour. However, the body and root of the matter derived from Pesach is not understood: why need an hour for roasting — it could be roasted on Shabbat itself at night, only time before Shabbat entry stuck in the oven, and Shabbetai has nothing in it, and so explained in the mishnah: they lower Pesach into the oven with darkening. If so, why need an hour time to advance the tamid order — perforce what is cited for roasting is not precise, only for preparing the roast, to gather wood (and they said at the beginning of Pesachim: one hour to gather wood and heat the oven, etc.), and if so perhaps Taharat HaAdam relied entirely; and perhaps Taharat HaAdam holds the hour time for Pesach roasting stated in the Gemara is precisely only what the Sages initially fixed when they fixed time for tamid and Pesach, they did not want to rely on this that Pesach would be roasted on Shabbat and lowered near darkening, only they fixed order and bound one hour time on erev Shabbat for roasting; and this is their statement only if it happened it was not roasted on erev Shabbat while day was still great, permitted to lower even on erev Shabbat with darkening, and not needed; but l'chatchila it was roasted on erev Shabbat and its time was one hour. However, even from this proof it follows Rambam's measure and the others who have changing measures for different mil walking, which is one-third hour, and for Rambam half hour approximately, for roasting that all its blood exits is if roasted like food of the Temple, and so below siman 73 if roasted like food of the Temple again blood exits — and this is clear that Pesach was roasted completely as fit for eating entirely, and as if holy food for thieves — see end of Pesachim that Pesach is written regarding it for anointing and greatness as kings eat, and therefore eaten on satiety — see there Rashba. Therefore if its entire roasting was an hour, then food of the Temple according to authorities who hold food of the Temple is one-third of its roasting — see below siman 112 — and if in one-third hour it was roasted so all blood exits, also in salting so, and one-third hour suffices, and all views flowed from Taharat HaAdam's proof — examine.`,
    '7#_': `In fruit juice — Rama in responsum wrote: from Ran's words regarding the slaughterer who did not find blood, it implies initial rinsing in fruit juice does not help, for they congeal it; and Taz, Shach, and Pri Chadash wondered: we find no such thing in Ran, only on Tosafot's question on "and they are eaten with soiled hands" that it was kashered through rinsing, and Ran answered it was rinsed in rainwater — see there; if so how did Rama learn from this regarding fruit juice, which they never mentioned? And it seems without intent for Rama, who lives by roasting — spoke without knowledge, Heaven forbid; so one should understand Ran: he holds rinsing in rainwater does not kasher them — behold they said in the mishnah: whatever begins or ends with intent is mekarev; here their intent is to rinse the meat and prepare it for eating, and there is no greater intent than this, and they are mekarev; and one must say his intent was to roast it, not for rinse water, and if so his intent was without moistening the meat in water and he is not pleased with this rinsing at all, and if so it is not for intent and they are not mekarev; but nevertheless, if when he salted in second rinsing in fruit juice it was kashered — if in rainwater this certainly pleases him that he cannot do without rinsing, then even rainwater is mekarev and his answer is nothing; and needless to say certainly second rinsing is discussed, that it was rinsed in fruit juice and rainwater is not needed, only initial rinsing that fruit juice does not help — difficult; and on this answer rainwater, and it is not pleasing to him that his intent was to eat it and they are not mekarev — examine.`,
    '9#א': `Even if it was not placed, etc. — before we stood on the root of this law, we must precede the law that Taz and Pri Chadash disagreed, for we say in chapter Kirah: melicha needs cooking like ox meat, for even in kli rishon it is not cooked except on fire; and Rosh brought it and wrote: however, if it was salted, even salted anew, although the meat is not cooked, nevertheless the moisture and salt and blood inside are cooked and absorbed in the meat and forbidden — end of his words. And the authors disagreed on Rosh's intent, for Rama in Torat Chatat holds even if it was not salted at all, nevertheless forbidden, for moisture cooks and "salted" Rosh cited is not precise; and proof: Tur, Orach Chaim siman 318 wrote forbidden to place live beef in kli rishon on Shabbat, for moisture cooks, and did not write "salted" — learn "salted" is not precise; and so ruled Orach Chaim siman 318, "salted" is not precise. However, Maharshal and Taz in Orach Chaim siman 318 s.k. 23 hold he did not write "salted" for nothing, only Rosh deals with blood matter — if so what of it that blood cooks, the meat does not absorb from blood, for it must cook on fire and until then it does not absorb; therefore Rosh requires "salted," for then salt absorbs blood. But for Shabbat law it is obvious moisture and blood cook without salt, and Tur in Orach Chaim forbade for Shabbat, did not mention salt — end of his words. And Pri Chadash thought strained that Rosh's words revolve there on salting for blood — what does this matter here, only deals with Shabbat issur, only although l'chatchila forbidden because he cooks moisture, nevertheless one may question why forbid b'dieved the piece that was not cooked, and if moisture cooked, wipe the moisture — therefore he said salt hastens to absorb and impossible to wipe — end of his words. And his words are strained, for what did Rosh want here saying salt and blood cooked — he should have said simply moisture; and how does salt cook, it is not cooked in kli rishon; and far-fetched in reality to wipe all moisture from every fiber and piece of meat — behold much moisture; and if he comes to wipe he must crumble meat to fine pieces indivisible; therefore according to plain language of Rosh it seems like Taz and Ri'az agrees, who wrote on a piece placed in kli rishon: permitted except if there is salt on it, and so Rosh — namely as Taz wrote. And truthfully one may question on Rosh, since he holds even if moisture cooks, nevertheless meat does not absorb in kli rishon — if so what does "salted" help to absorb, and is not heat of kli rishon more absorbing than salt? And if we say with salt and kli rishon heat they cause absorption — therefore Rosh needed to give reason moisture cooks, only briefly, even kli rishon does not cook meat but with salt combination it cooks — and one may resolve with strain. And after we explained these many views, it seems certain one who relies on Taz and Maharshal — they do not rebuke him with contempt, as I ruled some years ago when they sought to remove a posek's beard because he ruled beef placed in kli rishon hot water is not forbidden, and I wrote he has whom to rely on from great ones of the world. However, l'chatchila for law, requires further study to be lenient against Rama's ruling, for many reasons; and even if we say Rosh's intent as Taz, that although blood cooks, nevertheless not absorbed in meat, for kli rishon does not cook beef — Rosh followed regarding pouring after R' Tam's view in chapter Kirah regarding tray and pot, etc., who holds since he poured, absorbs a peel's depth, even cooking a little is one law — he has this view, and see Ran and Rashba in their novellae and Taharat HaAdam and Tosafot Zevachim; and if so Rosh inferred well: if kli rishon does not cook beef meat, also does not absorb from them; and therefore Rosh requires specifically the piece was salted. But according to Rashbam and many poskim, fire absorbs more than cooking, and pouring proves — if so what of it that beef is not cooked in kli rishon, blood is absorbed in kli rishon, fire absorbs — obvious; and so plain teachings of matters, absorption is not more than cooking, for certainly heat of salting is not more than place of kli rishon, as appears everywhere; if so if kli rishon does not help on beef, also heat of salting does not help on beef — therefore Rama's case that he salted slaughtered meat with nevelah meat, all should be in other meat; and so these atmata that he salted in gid hanasheh, etc. — this matter is hard to say; and therefore it is clear there is a difference between cooking and absorption, and heat of salting absorbs. Also I did not know whence Rosh said salt assists kli rishon — he found no support from Gemara and mentioned it; and even though moisture cooks, my heart was troubled whence Rosh innovated a law not found in Shas — these matters contradict nature — who can enter their secret, until I found proof from Gemara Zevachim: we say one who cooked in it or poured boiling into it — it breaks; and see Tosafot and Yerushalmi who learned from it pouring cooks, therefore breaking required as written "in which it shall be cooked," etc.; and Tosafot challenged: fine if he poured into the vessel — if the sin-offering was a calf and ox and he poured into the vessel, why break, behold it is better than full kli rishon, it does not cook; and so too pouring — perforce one must say moisture cooks, and so in pouring moisture cooks, and Scripture says "shall be cooked in it" and requires breaking; and why there mention salted, who mentioned salting — besides impossible to say salted there, besides whence for Rabbenu Tam and supporters to learn from there pouring cooks, perhaps there salting assists, and even in kli sheni some say it assists, all the more in pouring. But to innovate that salting assists kli rishon heat — I did not know whence; therefore more plausible like Rama's view, moisture cooks and is absorbed in meat, especially if not absorbed without salting — if so beef in milk and kli rishon, meat is not cooked and also its moisture is not absorbed — behold d'oraisa the meat is permitted, only d'rabbanan; and so here. If so, why do we challenge on Rava who said an olive's bulk of meat fell into a boiling pot, the meat is forbidden and the milk permitted — why is the milk nevelah, and we establish it with change — why not answer beef that is not forbidden only d'rabbanan and forbidden from absorbing milk, but milk is not forbidden from meat, for meat is permitted, not cooked at all, and in Bava Kama way it is forbidden; and answer: nevertheless d'rabbanan forbidden, behold in d'rabbanan we do not say issur chal al issur; and so one must say in what we answer "the Torah forbade the kid and not the milk" — we find nowhere milk from Bava Kama is permitted; and therefore the matter is forced, moisture cooks and is absorbed inside meat like Rama; and truthfully the Mechaber in siman 318 holds even moisture is not cooked without salt, and it does not appear so from Rambam who copied "melicha needs cooking" and omitted that ox meat needs cooking on fire and not kli rishon helps — why he omitted requires reason; but for Rama's view it is fine, for in any case moisture cooked and forbidden, and no difference for law, therefore they did not mention — and this is proof for Rama. And behold Ran in chapter HaRoeh wrote on that which we do not salt kids and lambs, challenged: if they are ox meat and need cooking, kli rishon heat does not cook them — obviously permitted to salt, unlike kids and lambs; and if we say milk nevertheless absorbs — Ran should not have answered that it expelled its blood, for for this kli rishon does not help; and one may say there is a difference between calf and ox, for the Gemara says ox meat — it cannot be we give a boundary how many days until ox status; who decides and judges this, and they said an ox one-day-old is called ox; therefore clear he holds moisture even without salt cooks, and Ran challenged well — examine. And in truth, although Rosh was lenient, we have to be stringent, for Rosh as appears from his words first chapter Chullin in the matter of slaughtering on Shabbat holds blood that spread place to place is permitted (and what he wrote in chapter Keitzad Tzolin regarding unperforated vessel that blood that spread place to place is forbidden is only Rif's language) — if so one may say even though kli rishon heat does not help on ox meat, nevertheless its heat is so great that at least it causes movement in blood to absorb to exit; and if so, although heat is not so strong as on fire to expel absorption outside, nevertheless blood spread to exit — and Rosh did not concern himself, for he holds permitted; but according to what we hold regarding unperforated vessel that the whole piece is forbidden because blood spread place to place, this too is similar for all — examine.`,
    '9#ב': `All the more in kli sheni it cooks — and Maharshal disagrees, for kli sheni does not cook; and Shach in s.k. 37 seems to agree with Maharshal and to rely on Rosh and poskim who wrote kli sheni cooks with salt combination — it is not so precise, and one may reject in several aspects, especially that which they wrote kli sheni cooks means other than beef, but for beef they agree even kli rishon does not cook, and Rosh deals with kli sheni and needs salt assistance; but if this is difficult, this is difficult — we teach: pot, etc., one may not pour boiling on them, but one may pour into the bowl and ladle; R' Yehudah says: for everything he puts except something with vinegar and brine; and they should have asked: does R' Yehudah argue on the first part leniently that even kli sheni requires vinegar and brine, or on the latter stringently that even kli sheni cooks with vinegar and brine leniently; and the sugya is established from R' Yehudah's baraita on the first part, that he holds kli sheni does not cook except with vinegar and brine — implying the first tanna and R' Yehudah who argue hold even with vinegar and brine and salting, kli sheni does not cook, only kli rishon; and we do not find dispute of first tanna and R' Yehudah on the latter part; and it is difficult for Ohr HaChaim and Rama: and one may say and distinguish between brine and brine — see Yerushalmi Shabbat chapter Kirah, that according to R' Yehudah salt is like wine brine like vinegar, and distinguish cooking from absorption — difficult, for Ohr HaChaim and Rama seized everything with language "cooked"; and what one may say, perhaps Ohr HaChaim's proof from Pesachim daf 40b: we do not smear the pot on Pesach, and one who wishes to smear puts the flour then vinegar, and one says even put vinegar then flour — who is the one who says? R' Chisda, R' Yehudah, who says for everything he puts except vinegar and brine — Rashi: for everything he puts spices in kli sheni except something with vinegar and brine that vinegar cooks them although prior — requires slight further study, for on Shabbat we establish R' Yehudah on the first part on kli rishon, not bowl and ladle which are kli sheni, as we establish from the baraita; and one must say smearing law is kli sheni law — if not, what does R' Yehudah ask, behold first tanna did not disagree with R' Yehudah on kli rishon, only holds it cooks even without vinegar and brine; and forced to say smearing, etc., is like kli sheni — and the question remains we do not find for R' Yehudah dispute on kli sheni as above; and one must say thus the baraita explanation: the one who says for all pots — the word "all" teaches both kli rishon and kli sheni, except something with vinegar and brine forbidden in all pots, both kli rishon and kli sheni, they do not disagree; if so, smearing deals with R' Yehudah who for vinegar and brine one law for kli rishon and kli sheni — this is what resolves Rashi's words, and still the question on Ohr HaChaim who ruled not like first tanna who disagreed with R' Yehudah. It seems he holds Tosafot Shabbat explained R' Yehudah argues on the first part not that kli rishon does not cook, only spices are hard to cook; if so, do not make a distant dispute — therefore first tanna argues with R' Yehudah that vinegar and brine do not cook in kli sheni, namely in hard spices; but in other soft things they agree it cooks, for even in hard spices he holds it cooks; and smearing is spices, therefore for first tanna it does not cook; and Ohr HaChaim deals with other things besides spices — strained. And more plausible: understand first tanna that even in brine and vinegar, kli sheni does not cook — why permit putting flour first then vinegar, for vinegar will cook them and prevent leavening? It must not act at all; and one must say they argue when there is vinegar and brine in the bowl in the dish and afterward one wants to put spices in it: first tanna permits, for vinegar and brine power was already nullified in the dish; R' Yehudah forbids nevertheless; but to put in kli sheni spices and pour vinegar with them immediately — vinegar combines with heat and cooks and is forbidden. And so in smearing, if he puts flour first then vinegar, vinegar cooks the flour so it not leaven — if so, from vinegar. And what appears in Ohr HaChaim's intent regarding salted meat placed in kli sheni — behold immediately it will nullify before combining with kli sheni heat and forbids. And in my novellae I said Rashi has proof for this, for the one who says for all pots means both kli rishon and kli sheni; Rashi holds like Rashbam that pouring does not cook — and examine Tosafot's question why teach "but one pours into the bowl" — teach a greater novelty, "but one pours boiling on them"; according to Rashi, to reconcile: because of R' Yehudah he cited even in the bowl with vinegar and brine it is forbidden, and if he taught pouring, one would say according to R' Yehudah he forbids in pouring with vinegar and brine, not in kli sheni — and he teaches even in this it is forbidden — examine.`,
    '9#ג': `All the more the salt is forbidden to eat — Shach in s.k. 41 wrote in the name of Efodi Rabrebbi that b'dieved when well dried it is permitted, and Shach left it in requires further study; and to me there is no doubt to forbid, for although Ran brought in the name of Ramban and other sages who hold the salt is permitted, for the blood was consumed and gone — none of them are proof, for they follow their line that we do not say issur vel heter in other issurim, and likewise it seems also squeezing is permitted according to Tosafot, for one depends on the other; if so, since absorbed blood was consumed and lost, obviously salt is permitted, for it was squeezed and lost. But according to what we hold in all issurim nevela, and likewise possible to squeeze it — forbidden; even for one who does not say issur vel heter — if so, this is because of blood absorbed in salt, not because of issur vel heter — behold the salt, even though absorbed blood was consumed, nevertheless: (1) to squeeze it is forbidden, and behold salting became forbidden from blood — how can you return it to permission; therefore there is no doubt it is forbidden.`,
    '10#_': `In ms"l't — behold, one should understand: blood in meat is species in a different species and can be clarified by tasting — if so, let the gentile taste for us, as explained below laws of mixtures; and what need for reliability to rely on his tasting? And strained to say: for whether it was rinsed we rely on him, but for reliability we need he is expert in distinction — not so from Mechaber, beginning siman 98 — see there; if so, why need reliability — give him and let him taste us. And one may say Ran, chapter Kol HaBashe, stood on what they said salting period mil — we see the mohal exiting from meat after this period much redder than the first; and answer: certainly salting comes only to remove blood on the meat's surface and near it; for absorbed blood in the piece we are not concerned, for it is blood of limbs that did not spread, permitted; and further answer: after salting period there is no blood, only mohal reddens, etc.; and according to this view every piece is full of blood, only they did not forbid except blood on the piece's surface, not what is in the essence of the piece; if so, how does he say he does not know from his tasting, for he does not feel taste of blood; if so, we judge it was rinsed — what of it, there is still blood from the congealing place; all the more this answer that it is mere mohal, certainly equal in taste to blood — if so, how do we rely on the gentile saying taste of blood? Truthfully as his words, and nevertheless it does not forbid, for it is mohal — so Rashba in his novellae to Chullin Bei Duga, for impossible for the drip to taste blood, for also fat drip tastes like blood and impossible to distinguish, and all the more meat mohal. And with this the challenge on Rama is resolved, who ruled even in d'rabbanan issurim we do not rely on ms"l't, and in this law he wrote one may rely on ms"l't — it is resolved, for here the piece is in its blood, since issur is only blood on top and inside the piece is permitted, it is majority permitted blood and species in species is nullified in majority permitted taste, or forbidden taste is nullified in majority permitted taste; or say when it cooks, added afterward water more than sixty against blood, and nevertheless issur vel heter forbids, but impossible to taste, for taste already nullified in sixty — if so, also concern that cooking them will give taste is gone, for taste of blood already nullified in more than sixty, and only taromet mixture of species in species remains — to reconcile, examine. And further one may say: here the gentile fears lest we taste him and the blood inside will be discovered, since he knows it was not rinsed and salted; also many poskim hold the piece itself sixty against absorbed blood, only disputants are not clear to me, and from uncertainty we do not extract; and there is here double safek safek: perhaps it was rinsed, and if you say not rinsed, perhaps there is sixty — see below general double safek that in such a case we do not invert; if so, at least ms"l't helps. And further one may say: here d'rabbanan, for Rama holds below siman 98 that even in a d'rabbanan matter ms"l't does not help — it is fine according to what I wrote, that here there are two d'rabbanan matters.`,
    '11#א': `Meat that was cooked — a woman cooked meat and forgot if she salted it first before cooking, and the Rav Taz permitted, for it is only d'rabbanan safek lekula; and also like safek in reading in the second chapter "that they may increase," etc., we say she is accustomed — so this woman in her habit certainly salted; and Rav author of Noda BiYehudah challenged, for itchezk issura; and see Pri Chadash. And behold, if itchezk issura, does d'rabbanan safek lekula help — see below general rules on double safek what I wrote there, and from there seek; and it seems if the woman attributes her error to something that is certainly without doubt why she forgot and how this safek came — it seems clear to answer we say in many places Pesachim 16: chaver died and left produce — they are in a presumption of being fixed, and the Gemara inferred from this safek removes certainty; and Tosafot Niddah 15b s.v. chaver wrote this safek removes certainty is a good safek, and he is accustomed, therefore it pushes off certainty; and the Gemara also said therefore presumption of being fixed, for certainly he does not remove from his hand what is not fixed; and also this too is safek that perhaps he put in refuse, etc. And clearly if we find a pot of cooked meat and the cook is not before us to ask if she salted the meat, obviously the meat is permitted to eat — we never heard she must ask a woman cook if she salted; and as with chametz, presumption the house was checked — all are colleagues regarding salting to salt meat; if so, behold safek removes certainty, even though presumption was initially not salted and safek it was salted — like presumption fixed to separate tithes from what I wrote to salt, and like there they permitted, so permit here, and there is no distinction at all. And if we say here is a flaw — how did she forget a great action of salting — say on the other side, how did safek arise whether she salted; what brought it to this safek? Say she did a great action; but go learn from safek whether he prayed and certainty he did not — he does not return to pray, prayer is d'rabbanan; although certainly obligated, if safek whether he prayed, how does certainty remove safek? Also how forget such great action whether he prayed — requires favor of heart; do they speak of feeble-hearted outwardly? All the more liable to remember salting which hands are busy all day. And if you say prayer is different lest he bless in vain — here concern for blessing in vain; challenge one explanation Pesachim that even in d'rabbanan safek does not remove certainty; if so why exempt prayer and Kriat Shema — strain that when they enacted d'rabbanan like prayer and Kriat Shema they enacted for certainty not safek; in any case in such a case safek removes certainty, and all the more according to Tosafot there that "his animal" is all d'oraisa, but d'rabbanan nevertheless obligated in tithes and certainly so, and Gemara concedes in d'rabbanan safek removes certainty. And Taz's words are clear. However, still in its place as Shach wrote, what is the alarm — take kafila to taste whether taste of blood, certainly she forgot, and if not certainly she did not forget; and because we are stringent not to rely on kafila in this we are lenient in the deed? One must say as I wrote, every piece is full of blood but they forbade only blood smeared on top; therefore even if taster tastes blood, no proof it was not salted; and according to what I wrote this is category safek removes certainty, no question, for he can clarify by asking her mouth; in any case no need to clarify, no need kafila tasting. And so Chullin 97, Gemara challenged: if so, if it fell into another pot it should not be forbidden; Tosafot: even though safek whether all exited in first pot or only part, d'rabbanan safek lekula. Understand: in falling into another pot that Gemara forbids, he tasted kafila — what d'rabbanan safek here, kafila tasting would decide; one must say Gemara's question where no kafila, and if we rely on d'rabbanan safek; if so even for us who do not rely on kafila, as if no kafila, rely on d'rabbanan safek — requires further study to be lenient, for one may reject: Gemara's question if pot increased now no milk taste, nevertheless forbidden since initially less than sixty and nevela; unlike if safek d'rabbanan lekula, never forbidden; but regarding tasting, in truth we do not rely — if possible to taste; therefore requires further study. Tzitz Eliezer brought in Kereti: if safek whether milk salted with meat — d'oraisa safek, species in different species forbidden; Pri Chadash agreed. And I say: if he peels meat — permitted, for milk spreads in salting per most poskim does not spread, peel suffices; see Turei Chayim general rule 38, below siman 105 Shach s.k. 33 many authorities hold does not spread more than peel; even stringent did not decide; certainly to spread until stops even in sixty requires removal; see siman 105. If so, double safek: perhaps no milk contact, and if contact perhaps does not spread more than peel; if unknown which place touched until peel place unknown, one in two nullified, see siman 92 Shach. What I wrote in Peleti, do not doubt. However as Shach wrote, milk in meat is species in different species, clarified by gentile tasting — how say lekula we do not rely on him? It seems from poskim salting taste is weak, impossible to taste; see Rambam Hilchot Maachalot Assurot 15:34 — salted nevelah mixed with slaughtered meat forbidden because salt became nevelah, etc., impossible to stand on taste or issur — requires further study. Tzitz Eliezer: if safek milk safek fat, species in species nullified d'oraisa, only d'rabbanan safek lekula; Pri Chadash questioned two safekot to be stringent, perhaps salted together, perhaps milk salted not fat — I did not understand: in the end safek whether milk salted — if not, permitted, d'rabbanan safek — what difference two safekot or not, why be stringent in d'rabbanan issur?`,
    '11#ב': `That piece — lest not all exited outside the piece, only spread place to place — and one should understand: nevertheless blood in cooking is d'rabbanan, if so d'rabbanan safek lekula, and itchezk issura did not apply at all, for from here we would say spread place to place — behold blood is absorbed in place. And strained to say reason they forbid: perhaps can be clarified through kafila and tasting — not mentioned in poskim. Therefore one may say certainly blood exited as nature of cooking, only we are concerned before exit it remained in the piece and spread place to place and the piece became nevela, and even after exit, possible to squeeze forbidden — therefore that piece remains in its issur, therefore kafila taste does not help, for it is not there, only exited exited; and this reasoning Tosafot wrote Chullin 96b s.v. vekhelev: chelev in chelev before it exited from chelev forbids the meat — the same here. And do not challenge: if so in every piece salted before all expulsion finished, spread place to place and nevela — say: even though melicha power to expel all blood is blood that did not spread, but to absorb taste of blood it is not absorbed through salish, only kli rishon, and issur vel heter does not apply.`,
    '12#_': `And one should not leave it, etc. — and that which they permit safek treifah poultry and even an egg that needs cooking, there they abandon it and it has no remnant, we did not decree lest he eat from it; but this meat he does not abandon, for it has whiteness in roasting — we decree; and further, there benefit in leaving it, for anyway safek will be clarified, or it will lay eggs and the like; but here what benefit in leaving for cooking — never will be permitted, and for roasting immediately permitted — if so, why need to leave and come to concern for mishap?`,
    '13#_': `Safek whether it was salted, etc. — and if the safek is in the animal when it was slaughtered, such as by way of parable day four meat before us and we do not know if slaughtered day one and three days passed, or day two and still within three days — say establish the animal on presumption of life, and now that it died; and so in sugya Niddah, Ketubot, Kiddushin, and many places: establish live on presumption of life; all the more if we saw it alive before — it is as if we saw it alive from evening. And in this I will answer: since it is only stringency of Geonim, they were lenient, for such is the law.`,
    '14#_': `And so the law if cooked without salting — Rav Shach challenged: behold it is blood with meat, species in different species, therefore not nullified dry in dry one in two, lest they cook them and it will give taste (and according to his line below siman 109 that even in d'rabbanan issur one in two is not nullified — see there); and from stringency of Geonim it is not difficult, for they were not so stringent; but on Rama who wrote if cooked 100 and it seems I preceded to challenge: perhaps clarified through kafila tasting, how is nullification relevant — two answers: either as Ran wrote every piece is full of blood, only they did not forbid only blood smeared on top, not inside, nevertheless blood, and mercy permitted — if so how will kafila taste taste permitted blood absorbed in piece, if so also Shach's question is resolved, for even if cooked will not give forbidden taste, for there is permitted blood taste and forbidden taste nullified in majority permitted; or say when he cooks he adds afterward water more than sixty against blood, and nevertheless issur vel heter forbids, but impossible to taste, for already nullified in sixty — if so also concern cooking them will give taste is gone; examine.`,
    '15#_': `Bloody meat, etc., for roasting — Shach challenged how will roasting help on filth that is visible blood and kevush is like cooking, certainly sixty without doubt. And I wrote: every piece sixty against visible blood on the piece; and if they said so for blood in second rinsing, all the more in first rinsing, known that more blood smeared in second than first, and piece kevush in water on all sides cannot be unless water more than piece — behold here twice thirty against blood nullified on all sides — to reconcile. However, in the body of the matter how will roasting help after soaking, since kevush is like cooking, and Pri Chadash challenged Mechaber himself seif 18 ruled in unperforated vessel what was kevush in brine, roasting does not help — however on this question one may say Mechaber is uncertain whether kevush is fully like cooking and roasting does not help, or not, and here that it soaked in water many hold in water is not kevush specifically in vinegar and brine, and many hold only three days; therefore he combined this with that and permitted roasting, but below in brine he did not want to rely on the lenient view that roasting helps in kevush alone; but the essence of the law needs teaching what roasting helps — it seems Mechaber holds kevush acts to cook to expel or absorb, but to expel and absorb together is judged like kevush like boiling pot that did not cool from its soaking and is like a pot that did not cool from boiling, and many poskim hold it only acts to expel or absorb, not both together — therefore they are lenient in roasting, unlike below seif 20 where brine full of blood is before you and piece is kevush in it and soaking acts to absorb brine blood and one action makes clear forbidden, therefore roasting does not help.`,
    '16#א': `Sixty against peel from bowl — see Shach and Pri Chadash, and from their words we learn the bowl is not forbidden, only a peel's depth, for melicha does not forbid more than a peel's depth — needs teaching: behold brine that remained inside long enough to put on fire and boil — if so, it is kevush and forbids all of it as Shulchan Aruch wrote seif 18; and if there is no melicha for vessels, this means to expel, but not to absorb — and one must say kevush does not apply here, for kevush is called when brine surrounds on all sides, unlike here the vessel is surrounded outside by air, only brine inside the vessel and vessel surrounds it — not judged as kevush, only as mere salting — requires further study, for Magen Avraham siman 447 s.k. 97 extended and concluded in salting one should be stringent that if it remained long enough to boil in brine it expels from the vessel, meaning it is kevush even though outside is not kevush; and so if water remained in the vessel beforehand Ohr HaChaim holds it is kevush — see below siman 105; even though vessel walls outside are not kevush, compelled from many places from that which bowl in which one salted meat — why should it be forbidden, behold there is no melicha for vessels, as Rosh wrote regarding cheese molds — perforce must be from kevush reason, and cheese molds deals with holes, no soaking here — requires further study.`,
    '16#ב': `Peel — and Shach challenged why require peel from vessel and not peel from hot thing placed in it, ta'tei gavar, as Rama wrote in Torat Chatat — one may say since many authors hold the bowl is judged as hot, for brine was absorbed in it; if so, even for those who hold it is called cold, meaning brine in its place has no power to heat the bowl, nevertheless brine in its place stands, it is boiling, and when pouring boiling into it, obviously heat awakens heat, for this heat is certainly stronger than kli sheni heat, for this is at least kli rishon cooking, and all the more does not cook at all; nevertheless Rama wrote above in kli sheni with brine combination it cooks — if so, all the more in irui it cooks; therefore require peel against the whole vessel, not suffice with food peel — with this Rashi's question is resolved in chapter Keitzad Tzolin daf 111b, bowl in which one salted meat, perhaps taste through kafila, and Rashba challenged: behold it is not forbidden in pouring boiling into it, only a peel's depth from the food — if so, what taste in the matter that requires only a peel; and according to the above it is fine, for the whole food is forbidden and he challenged well on taste, etc. — examine.`,
    '16#ג': `Even without perforation — Derishah challenged: if it remained long enough to put on fire, behold it is kevush like cooking — answer: regarding vessel we do not equate brine kevush, for there is no melicha for vessels — this contradicts Magen Avraham's words in siman 447, clear also in vessel we say brine kevush long enough to put on fire — see there; if so, for Magen Avraham Derishah's question stands in its place. And one should understand that which cheese molds of gentiles — reason of those who permit: there are holes; and assumption: those who forbid, sometimes holes are plugged — unperforated vessel — learn in vessel there is soaking, etc., therefore distinguish between plugged holes and unblocked holes, then no soaking — learn kevush applies. Therefore it seems one should distinguish: if piece in salt and brine did not yet drip from it, no issur in vessels, for there is no melicha for vessels; but if brine dripped from it, brine equates it to boiling and it is also kevush in the vessel; and in Shulchan Aruch here it deals where brine did not drip; and one may be stringent; and in any case Magen Avraham's words are correct, for in cheeses where Maharai permitted molds and gave reason all issur absorbed is only d'rabbanan, cheese, etc., impure milk is not common — see Yesh Seder LaHalachah in Keitzad Tzolin; but in Maharai's case, chametz d'oraisa issur, Maharai concedes to forbid — see there. And do not challenge Magen Avraham's lines that in vessel there is soaking — that which we say Chullin 111b, there is no source for Ravin's statement melicha is not like rotev, for this pan in which they salt meat in it broke R' Ami, etc., and challenged: whence for us from Torah of salting it broke, perhaps only from Torah of kevush that they salted in it and remained long enough to put on fire and it is kevush and therefore it broke; and if Ravin also holds kevush is not like cooking, perhaps he holds kevush is not like cooking, only like rotev roasting, and melicha is not like rotev — must say the Gemara knew this pan incident, it did not remain time long enough to put on fire, nevertheless R' Ami broke, and it is clear. And one may say: whence does the Gemara say for Ravin kevush is not like cooking from this pan — it is not proven, only melicha is like rotev; whence for us to reject Ravin's words that it is not cooked; and one may say certainly he holds kevush is not cooking, and how will he disagree several mishnayot that kevush cooks in seeds — must say soaking beforehand he concedes it cooks, but brine kevush long enough to put on fire, etc., he holds it does not cook; and one must say the reason: there is no boiling thing that boils it until it is complete soaking — fine if melicha is like rotev, behold a thing that boils through remaining long enough to put on fire is kevush; but if melicha is not like rotev and it is cold and only because it remained a while it becomes soaking cooked — if so, one depends on another, if melicha is not like rotev, also brine kevush is not like cooking — examine.`,
    '18#א': `And it remained, etc. — implies even if it did not remain salting period for cooking, nevertheless called boiling; and below siman 91 it seems before this time not called boiling — per what I wrote to distinguish between blood and other absorptions; however one may say even though not boiling, nevertheless at time of soaking it forbids, and vinegar proves it is not like rotev to absorb, nevertheless makes it kevush; and the same here, not called boiling, nevertheless makes it kevush. Only if not, question above on soaking that requires peel — why, behold not called boiling, and one must say deals where before putting in vessel he salted in unperforated vessel and remained time, afterward put in unperforated vessel and remained long enough to put on fire, etc., it is boiling brine, for in truth it remained salting period in combination of two vessels; and truthfully the law does not imply somewhat so, nevertheless for law it is correct.`,
    '18#ב': `Only a peel's depth — and if not known which side was placed inside the brine, the whole piece is forbidden — Pri Chadash; and it seems there is safek perhaps majority of piece was inside brine, but if clear to us there was not a majority, then majority nullifies, for one in two strictly is not required, only majority — see below laws of mixtures.`,
    '18#ג': `And there are those who forbid the whole piece, for blood was aroused to exit, etc., and it is blood that spread place to place — and one may challenge that which we say fish and birds salted one with another are forbidden, and Gemara challenged: if in unperforated vessel, why fish and birds, birds too; what is the question, Ravin who holds melicha is not like rotev concedes salt expels blood, for nature of salt is to expel blood — whence does Ravin need melicha for cooking, if so he concedes according to Rif's view in unsalted unperforated vessel it is forbidden, for blood was aroused to exit, etc.; if so the Gemara wanted to teach not like Ravin, melicha is truly like rotev to absorb and expel — but if it said birds and birds, one would say forbidden because blood spread place to place, but melicha is not rotev to absorb to another place, not like roasting — if so it teaches much; and answer: even birds and birds is explained thus, not simply even birds too, but in two ways: birds that expelled blood and birds that did not expel blood — one forbids its fellow because of absorption — learn melicha is like rotev. However this may be rejected: we teach even fish that did not expel at all, we do not say since they are busy it is forbidden — one may say.`,
    '18#ד': `And so is the practice — Pri Chadash wondered above siman 23 s.k. 2 on Rosh's words in chapter Keitzad Tzolin who drags after Rabbenu Peretz's words that blood spread place to place is forbidden, and in first chapter Chullin in matter of cooking on Shabbat he wrote like Tosafot that it is permitted — answer: in chapter Keitzad Tzolin also according to Rif, and in his words he does not hold only like Hagahot, and Rabbenu Peretz's practical case in unperforated vessel is R' Tam who forbade a chicken entirely that was salted in unperforated vessel, and R' Tam as Tosafot wrote in his name in matter of cooking on Shabbat wrote explicitly permitted regarding spread place to place — a) and contradicts — requires further study. And to resolve, it seems to distinguish in roasting, since fit to eat in its roasting, the blood that spread is blood in roasting which is d'rabbanan, only forbidden, and they were not stringent on blood that spread; and on this R' Tam's words refer to roasting that was not fully roasted, but in Rif's practical case of chicken immediately when placed in unperforated vessel blood immediately began to spread and it had no immediate outlet, being in unperforated vessel — examine; then still it was not salted blood and its issur is before salting, forbidden because of blood before salting, unlike in roasting it was roasted first — examine.`,
  },
};
